  event log, replay-safe IDs, conflict-tolerant shapes — for a server nobody has committed to building. That's
  speculative work, and if the server never arrives we've carried the cost for nothing.

  I'd also rule out calling the single-user version "a notifications system." Technically buildable today, but if only
  you can change a task, then "notified when tasks you care about change" collapses into a toast that says [3myou just did
[0m  [3mthe thing you just did[0m. The only genuinely useful piece left is due-date reminders — which is a real feature, but a
  much smaller and different one, and I'd want us to call it that honestly rather than ship it under the notifications
  banner.

  Which leaves the multi-user reading — the one where the feature means what it sounds like. And there my honest
  recommendation is uncomfortable: [1mnotifications shouldn't be the next thing you build.[0m They're a downstream consumer of
  three things this repo doesn't have — a task model, a notion of who a user is, and a source of change events.
  Building the notification layer first means inventing all three implicitly, badly, as a side effect. If that's the
  direction, I'd decompose into separate spec-and-plan cycles — tasks, then identity, then notifications — and we'd
  brainstorm the [3mfirst[0m one now.

  So I'm not going to pick for you, because the three paths differ in [3mhow much you're signing up to build[0m, and that's
  yours to decide. But I can ask the question that settles it without requiring any architecture opinion:
